# Sinfonia No. 9 in F minor, BWV 795 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: the chromatic lament. Three ideas are combined throughout,
# each moving between all three voices:
#   - the lament: a bass line falling by semitones in quarter notes
#     (F, E, Eb, D, Db, C), the old passus duriusculus;
#   - a sighing figure of paired eighths separated by rests
#     (F Ab G, G Bb A);
#   - a figure with a diminished leap and a tied note (Bb E F, tied).
# It is the slowest and most intimate of the Sinfonias. It should breathe,
# stay soft, and never push forward.
#
# Time signature: the score (Mutopia LilyPond) is in common time, 4/4,
# as the MIDI says. 35 bars, bar numbers here match both.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 40 (eighth = 80). 35 bars of 4/4 is 140 quarters.
# Leonhardt (harpsichord) takes 3:48, about 37 to the quarter; Schiff
# (Decca) 3:21, about 42; Gould (1964) 4:18, about 33. 40 lies inside
# that range, a little quicker than Leonhardt, so the long chromatic
# lines still connect on the chip, whose notes fade.
#
# Structure:
#   1-2    lament in the bass, sighs in the alto (soprano silent)
#   3-4    lament in the alto (C, B, Bb, A, Ab, G), sighs in the soprano
#   5      cadence in C minor; bars 5-6 bass silent
#   7-8    lament in the soprano, sighs in the bass, F minor
#   9-12   sequence; lament in the bass (11-12)
#   13     cadence in A-flat major, the relative major; lament in the
#          alto (13-14), sighs in the soprano
#   15     brief cadence in E-flat; bars 15-17 the bass climbs in sighs
#   18-19  lament in the soprano, C minor
#   20     cadence in C minor
#   21-25  sequences through B-flat minor; lament in the soprano (24-25)
#   26-27  lament in the alto
#   28     cadence on A-flat; bars 28-31 the bass climbs in sighs again
#   31-32  lament in the alto
#   33-34  the opening returns: lament in the bass, as at bar 1
#   35     final F with a major third (A natural), Picardy third
# The key areas are as read from the score; the precise cadence at bar
# 26 is ambiguous and is not marked here.

tempo 40

voice mid 0       # the alto carries the lament as often as the outer
                  # voices, so no standing step back for the middle voice

dyn 1 p           # bars 1-12: the lament stated, quiet throughout
dyn 13 mp         # bars 13-19: A-flat major, a little warmer
dyn 20 p          # bars 20-27: after the C minor cadence, back down
dyn 28 mp         # bars 28-35: after the A-flat cadence the bass climbs
                  # and the lament returns in the bass at 33

lead 1-2 bottom   # bars 1-2: lament in the bass
lead 3-4 mid      # bars 3-4: lament in the alto
lead 7-8 top      # bars 7-8: lament in the soprano
lead 11-12 bottom # bars 11-12: lament in the bass
lead 13-14 mid    # bars 13-14: lament in the alto (voices cross here,
                  # so the pitch ranking will not catch every note)
lead 18-19 top    # bars 18-19: lament in the soprano
lead 24-25 top    # bars 24-25: lament in the soprano
lead 26-27 mid    # bars 26-27: lament in the alto
lead 31-32 mid    # bars 31-32: lament in the alto
lead 33-34 bottom # bars 33-34: the return, lament in the bass

rit 1.5 0.75      # from the middle of bar 34 into the final chord
